The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued”. The
rendered cause table disagrees. A month-first parser and a misleading form hint need
separate tickets and separate fixes. A queued parser fix does not fix the hint.

## Engineering update · 5 October 2026

The course's engineering update reports **#4471 closed 05 oct**:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. [Issue 1](https://github.com/jeszdusiciel/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) closes as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** `booking-v3 form retired`. [Issue 2](https://github.com/jeszdusiciel/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) closes as not planned. Retirement removes that form; it is not a hint correction.

The analysis below describes the earlier export. The received/merged timeline cannot
verify either engineering outcome: the export stops before the update. These closure
reasons come from the course page, not from a production test performed here.

## What the data shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Counts use unique report IDs merged into this ticket, then join each report to its
tenant's locale and booking-form version. Browser locale and intake tags are guesses,
not the tenant's date convention. The table is computed when this page is built.

<ReviewEvidence />

## Two problems, two fixes

**Parser:** the shared parser reads month first even for day-first tenants. For example,
R-41200 describes a 7 April reminder arriving for 4 July. Parse using the tenant's
explicit format, reject invalid dates, and preserve date-only values without a timezone
conversion. Cover both app and email paths and both booking-form versions in regression
checks. The parser ticket includes the five uncertain reports listed below as filed
associations, not five proven instances of month/day reversal.

**Hint:** booking-v3 shows `MM/DD/YYYY` to day-first tenants while its form reads the
tenant's day-first format. Users following the hint get the wrong date even if the
shared parser is fixed. Generate the displayed hint and the form's interpretation from
one tenant format. Check v3 form submissions separately from v2 forms and app/email.

The public repository's `tickets/parser.md` and `tickets/hint.md` hold the ticket bodies;
each lists all assigned report IDs so its count can be audited.

## Read the uncertainty, too

The five reports in `desk-notes.json` do not establish another shared cause. R-41202
and R-41620 describe a one-day shift, R-41344 differs between Lisbon and Toronto,
R-41209 affects June, and R-41236 claims an invite preceded its booking. They are filed
under parser by the supplied tenant/channel rule. That rule does not prove their cause.
Keep the originals attached and investigate timezone handling, stored dates, event IDs,
and send timestamps. Do not invent a third fix from a common subject or a vague symptom.

## The queue is still working

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received time and merge time describe different events. The July merge spike does not
date the start of the failure, and “known” does not mean fixed. Keep both tickets open,
route new reports by the failing path, and monitor received counts separately from
merge counts. The update above now closes the tickets for different reasons; retain
their counts and evidence. Recheck a fresh export before treating this snapshot as current.

## Change what the desk does next

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) matched subjects alone.
Its consequences mention splitting one bug across subjects but omit the reverse:
unrelated bugs can share a subject and lose their own ticket. Its review date is old;
the live explorer already uses cause, not subject.

Use [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause), which replaces that rule without
rewriting the accepted historical record. Read the report, resolve the tenant, locale,
form and channel, and compare the failing path. Same subject is neither necessary nor
sufficient. Missing context or contradictory symptoms require investigation, not an
automatic merge. Reconcile counts after each new export, keeping one ID per report.

This review covers the supplied export through 4 October 2026 and records the course's
5 October engineering update. Later reports and independent production verification
are outside the evidence supplied here.

